      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "# The Lay of the Land\n\nThe Toronto Maple Leafs' season has mercifully ended - a 32-36-14 record, 78 points, eighth place in the Atlantic Division, and a seven-game losing streak that felt less like a skid and more like a mercy killing. <strong>This franchise finished 15th in the playoff race, 31 games behind the division leader, a chasm that no late surge could bridge.</strong> For a market that demands excellence and a roster built to contend, this is rock bottom masquerading as a wake-up call. The Leafs didn't collapse in the postseason. They never got there. They collapsed all season long - inconsistently, maddingly, predictably. The front office must now confront hard truths: whether the current core can coexist, whether the coaching staff is equipped to manage talent, and whether this roster construction was ever viable. <strong>Expect significant roster turnover this offseason and a searching evaluation of every pillar.</strong> The Leafs have the draft capital and cap flexibility to rebuild around their stars, but time is running out. Next season must look different, or the patience of a fanbase that has endured 19 years without a playoff series win will finally run dry.",
        "updated": "Updated May 15, 2026"
      },
