        "body": "The hard part of the spring is over; now comes the accounting. The Commanders have moved from draft-room dreams to roster math, and that is where good plans either harden or crack. <strong>The most important development is the arrival of the rookie class and the undrafted hopefuls, the fresh bodies who will decide which old names survive June.</strong> Washington sits 3rd in the NFC East, 6 games back, with a 1-game winning streak tucked into the ledger and a 14th-place playoff seed that means nothing now except reminder of how much ground was lost. That's the backdrop for the real work: finding immediate help, not headline theater. The draft haul has to fit the scheme, the depth chart, and the coach's patience all at once - especially in the trenches and on the back end, where young players can either stabilize a room or expose it. The Commanders don't need slogans. They need contributors. They need rookies who can tackle, cover, and line up without a seminar. <strong>What to watch next is simple: who separates in rookie camp, because those first reps will tell you whether this class is garnish - or dinner.</strong>",
